hello this is presh talwalkar  here are five facts that everyone should  know about the mathematical constant pi  fact number one the definition  pi is the ratio of the circumference of  a circle  to its diameter  that is pi equals the circumference  divided by the diameter  what's special is this is true for every  single circle whether the circle is very  big  or whether the circle is very small  pi will always be the ratio of the  circumference  to the diameter for every single circle  fact number two  pi is irrational  pi is equal to approximately 3.1415  and so on  the decimal form continues on forever  and it never repeats  the fraction 22 over 7  is accurate to 2 decimal places and the  fraction 355 over 113 is accurate to six  decimal places  but because pi is a rational  there is no fraction a over b where a  and b are integers that will be exactly  equal to pi  pi is also transcendental  which means pi will never be the root or  the solution of a non-constant  polynomial with integer coefficients  fact number three  archimedes estimated pi more than two  thousand years ago  archimedes considered a circle which had  a diameter of one  the circumference of this circle  will be equal to pi  so archimedes thought  he could estimate the circumference of  the circle  by considering drawing a hexagon inside  of the circle  the perimeter of this hexagon will be  clearly less than the circumference of  the circle which is pi  archimedes also consider drawing a  hexagon around the circle  now the perimeter of this hexagon will  be larger than the circumference of the  circle  so we can have an upper bound for pi  using trigonometry archimedes could  calculate the perimeter of the hexagon  and estimate pi  he then ingeniously doubled the number  of sides and considered the same  argument for a 12 gun  he doubled the number of sides again and  considered the argument for a 24 gone  he could calculate these perimeters of  the inscribed and circumscribed  perimeters to get  and lower bound and upper bound for pi  he got all the way to the 96 god in  which he found that pi must be wedged  between 223 over 71 and 22 over 7.  this gave him an approximation that pi  is about 3.14  which was very good and often practical  fact number four pi can be expressed in  many infinite series  this is my favorite infinite series pi  over four equals one minus one-third  plus one-fifth minus one-seventh plus  one-ninth and so on as the alternating  sum  of odd fractions  this is the leibniz formula which was  also discovered by the indian  mathematician madhava  another formula is pi squared over six  is equal to one over one squared plus  one over two squared plus one over three  squared and so on  this formula was found by euler  and it's a beautiful relationship  between the numbers and pi  there are also many complicated infinite  series this is one similar to one the  indian mathematician ramanujan would  have come up with and these types of  formulas are used in the shidnovsky  algorithm which is how pi is determined  algorithmically on computers  fact five  pi comes up in probability  here's a drawing of the normal curve or  the bell curve which you've seen in  statistics class to describe many  different  data  the equation of the curve is e to the  power of minus one half x squared  divided by the square root of 2 pi  so even in probability pi is a very  important constant and it comes up  consider buffon's needle problem  imagine randomly dropping a needle of  length l  on a floor where the lines are one unit  apart  what's the probability the needle will  intersect with one of the lines  the answer is two times the length of  the needle divided by  pi so unexpectedly pi comes up even in  probability  i hope you like this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can get my  books listed on my website follow my  blog mind your decisions  follow my decisions on facebook on  google plus  catch me on 